# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the earliest Long Count date 36 BC (Stela 2, Chiapa de
# Corzo), the Classic cities about AD 250 to 900, Chichen Itza 830 to 950, the Dresden Codex about
# 1200 to 1345. The numerals and calendars are from Wikipedia's Maya numerals and Maya calendar.
lesson history-the-maya v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The Maya: cities, numbers and calendars"
  goal "Read numbers written the Maya way, in twenties, work with their calendars, and place the Maya in time beside the rest of the world."

  look {
    scene 16x6 {
      mayanumber m values=[7, 13, 20] places=true letters=false at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      The Maya lived in the forests of what is now southern Mexico, Guatemala and Belize, where
      more than six million Maya people live today. From about AD 250 to 900 they built stone
      cities, grew maize and carved dates on stone. They counted in twenties: a dot is one, a bar is
      five and a shell is nought, and each level up the page is worth twenty times the one below.
      Here are 7, 13 and 20. They also kept two calendars at once, turning together like wheels.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.maya-read k=0
    }
    show history.maya-read k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show history.maya-which k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show history.maya-calendar k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.maya-order k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show history.maya-between k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.maya-codex k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.maya-read k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=7
    show history.maya-between k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show history.maya-order k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.maya-why-shell k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.maya-thousand k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "The Maya counted in twenties: a dot is one, a bar is five, a shell is nought, and each level up is worth twenty times more."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is a people of the Americas around AD 900 met through what they left: numbers, dates
    and books. The way in reads small numerals; the core reads numbers of two and three levels,
    works the calendars, and puts Maya dates beside the Qin, Rome, the Diamond Sutra and the Vikings.
    The two-star question asks why an empty level needs the shell, which is the idea of a zero that
    holds a place; the step up from Ashoka's digits in grade four is a place-value system in
    twenties rather than tens, with a zero of its own. The wrong idea to watch for is reading every
    mark as ones, so 26 comes out as 7. If the child is stuck, build the numerals with buttons and
    sticks on two sheets of paper, ones below and twenties above. Off the page, find Mexico,
    Guatemala and Belize on a map. The dates are in the lesson's record with their sources, and
    the Maya living today are from Wikipedia's Maya civilization page. England's key stage 2 history
    asks for "a non-European society that provides contrasts with British history: one study chosen
    from: early Islamic civilization, including a study of Baghdad c. AD 900; Mayan civilization c.
    AD 900; Benin (West Africa) c. AD 900-1300".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example reads 7, the numbers are below twenty, the numeral to find is 12, the order and the years are BC, and the calendar is 13 times 20."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the numbers have three levels with a shell between, the numeral to find is 405, the years run across year one to the Vikings, and the three-star question asks for 365 written in twenties."
  }
}
